    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and all 3 tests pass.\n\n- **Server:** it uses the pinned mcp 2.2.0 API, `MCPServer(\"notes\")` from `mcp.server`. In this version that class replaces `FastMCP`. The two tools are registered with `@server.tool()`.\n- **`add_note`:** stores the body in `NOTES[title]` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- **`word_count`:** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`. For an unknown title it raises `ToolError(\"no such note: <title>\")`. The client gets `is_error=True` with that message, so the calling model can read it. Using `ToolError` matters: the SDK treats any other exception as a crash and the model would only see `Error executing tool word_count`.\n- **stdio:** `python -m app.server` still calls `build_server().run()`, which serves over stdio by default. `python -m app.smoke` starts the server that way and lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n\nI also added `tests/test_tools.py`, which calls the tools through the SDK's in-process client. It checks that adding a note returns \"saved\", that the word count is correct, and that an unknown title comes back as a tool error containing \"no such note\". No dependencies were added or changed."
